# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files for more about ignoring files.
#
# If you find yourself ignoring temporary files generated by your text editor
# or operating system, you probably want to add a global ignore instead:
#   git config --global core.excludesfile '~/.gitignore_global'

# Ignore bundler config.
/.bundle

# Ignore the default SQLite database.
/db/*.sqlite3
/db/*.sqlite3-journal

# Ignore all logfiles and tempfiles.
/log/*
!/log/.keep
/tmp/*
!/tmp/.keep

# Ignore uploaded files in development.
/storage/*
!/storage/.keep

# Ignore assets.
/public/assets/

# === SECURITY: Environment and Secret Files ===
# Environment files (comprehensive protection)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template

# Rails secrets and encryption keys
config/master.key
config/credentials.yml.enc
config/credentials/
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.p7b
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore

# Session and encryption keys
.session.key
session.key
encryption.key
*session*.key

# Secret material — match by file SHAPE, not any path containing the substring
# "secret". Source code named "secret*" (e.g. Security::SecretStore, SecretFormModal)
# is NOT a secret and must stay tracked. Real secret files are caught here by shape
# and by the specific rules above/below (.env*, *.key, *.pem, credentials.yml.enc,
# .kamal/secrets, tmp/*secret*). .gitleaks.toml is the content-based backstop.
*.secret
*secret*.key
*secret*.pem
*secret*.pfx
*secret*.p12
*secret*.enc
*secret*.env
*secret*.yml
*secret*.yaml
secrets.json
# Private key material by SHAPE, per this block's rule above — NOT the bare
# substring `*private*`, which this line replaces (IMP-70a6c8b3b375). That
# pattern matched any path containing "private" anywhere, so a legitimately
# named source file was ignored with no error and no git status entry: it ate
# the first draft of server/spec/lint/extension_namespace_ratchet_spec.rb, and
# it is why `!/server/Gemfile.private` below had to exist at all.
#
# It was NOT what protects the private extensions — the anchored
# `/extensions/private/` + `extensions/private` pair below does that.
#
# MEASURED before narrowing, with `git check-ignore -v --no-index`. The
# --no-index matters: without it a TRACKED path reports "not ignored" whatever
# the rules say, so an attribution sweep run without it structurally cannot see
# rules covering tracked files. Over the 2,699 tree paths containing "private",
# exactly SEVEN were won by `*private*`:
#   - extensions/private itself           -> now the bare `extensions/private`
#   - 5 vendored gem files under
#     {server,worker}/vendor/bundle/      -> server/.gitignore:94, worker/.gitignore:4
#   - 1 spec inside the extensions/system SUBMODULE, which is tracked there and
#     for which a submodule's own git never consults this file. Not protected by
#     this rule before, not exposed by removing it.
# No path in the tree loses protection.
#
# ACCEPTED RISK, stated because it is real and not visible in today's tree:
# `*private*` had no slash, so it also blanket-ignored any DIRECTORY whose name
# contained "private" — extensions/private-business/, server/vendor/private-thing/,
# docs/private/. That accidental second net is gone. A private extension placed
# anywhere other than extensions/private/ is now trackable, so put them there.
#
# The common key extensions (*.key *.pem *.p12 *.pfx *.jks) are already ignored
# unconditionally above; `*private*key*` covers the extension-less and .txt
# shapes those miss (privatekey.txt, private_key, private-key, privatekeys).
*.private
*private*key*
*private*keys*
*private*.gpg
*private*.asc
*.token
*.tokens
api_keys.yml
api_keys.json
credentials.json
auth.json

