# Auto-generated docs — split policy:
#   TRACKED (committed):    mcp-tools.md (sourced from static code registry,
#                           regenerated manually via mcp:generate_tool_catalog)
#   IGNORED (local-only):   skills, knowledge-base, knowledge-graph, learnings,
#                           todo — DB-backed, regenerated nightly by
#                           AiKnowledgeDocSyncJob, account-specific. Querying
#                           the live state via MCP is the right read path.
# See docs/reference/auto/manifest.yml for the regeneration map. Every
# tracked auto-gen file carries an <!-- AUTO-GENERATED --> header enforced
# by docs/.verify/check-auto-gen-headers.sh — never hand-edit.
docs/reference/auto/skills.md
docs/reference/auto/knowledge-base.md
docs/reference/auto/knowledge-graph.md
docs/reference/auto/learnings.md
docs/reference/auto/todo.md

# RSpec auto-generated example files
worker/spec/examples.txt

# Ad-hoc test scripts with potential secrets
server/scripts/test_*.sh
server/scripts/trigger_*.sh

# Test output artifacts
**/rspec_results.json

# Per-host extension enable/disable state (written by admin "Toggle Extension")
config/extensions_state.json
# Private/custom extensions live under extensions/private/ (added manually by
# maintainers). Never committed — this repo publishes to a PUBLIC GitHub mirror.
#
# THIS ANCHORED RULE IS THE PROTECTION, on its own. An older comment here said
# "the *private* security rule above already ignores it", which was misleading
# in both directions: that rule was a bare substring match in the SECRET-material
# block (so it also ate ordinary source files named "private*"), and this line is
# what actually wins for every path under the directory. `*private*` was narrowed
# to key shapes in IMP-70a6c8b3b375; the measurement and the one risk that
# narrowing accepts are recorded at that rule.
# server/spec/lint/gitignore_substring_overreach_spec.rb pins both halves so
# neither can regress.
/extensions/private/
# Non-directory form as well. A trailing-slash pattern matches DIRECTORIES only,
# so if extensions/private is ever a SYMLINK (git sees a non-directory) the rule
# above does not fire and the link entry itself becomes trackable — committing
# the private extension's NAME, which the Gate-#9 note below treats as disclosure
# in its own right. The old `*private*` covered this by accident; this does it on
# purpose.
extensions/private
# Maintainer-local notes (never committed to the public repo)
/CLAUDE.local.md
# Gate-#9 grandfather entries whose PATH is inside extensions/private/. The tracked
# baseline beside it is published to the PUBLIC mirror, where such a path would
# disclose a private extension's name and internal layout. Written by
# scripts/generate-core-purity-baseline.sh; read by the hook and the scan mirror.
/.claude/hooks/core-purity-baseline.local.txt
# Private-mode bundle lock (declares private-extension PATH gems — never commit; see server/Gemfile.private)
/server/Gemfile.private.lock
# The private-mode Gemfile itself IS committed (tiny; just flips the discovery
# flag). Un-ignore it: `*.private` above matches it by extension. This negation
# predates that narrowing — it existed because the broader `*private*` caught it
# — and is still load-bearing, so do not drop it as leftover.
!/server/Gemfile.private
# Runtime-generated maintenance page: MaintenanceController writes it when
# maintenance mode is enabled and deletes it when disabled. Not a source file.
/server/public/maintenance.html

# Worker-generated report artifacts (regenerable; may contain PII in real deployments)
worker/storage/

# ui-smoke generated reports (regenerated each run)
scripts/ui-smoke/last-report.*
